      <h2>The 48-Hour Triage: Why "Informal Office Chats" Are Formal Interrogations</h2>
      <p>Over 64% of accused students are convicted before a formal hearing ever takes place simply because of panic responses. When a professor or conduct officer invites you to a "quick chat" or "informal coffee," they are conducting an official investigative interview. University officials record verbal concessions, nervous explanations, and courtesy apologies as formal, binding admissions of guilt.</p>
      
      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Strategic Rule:</strong> Never sign a summary resolution or make verbal admissions during an initial investigator meeting. Always invoke your right to review the evidence and respond in writing.
      </div>

      <h2>The F-1 Visa and SEVIS Immigration Threat</h2>
      <p>For international students holding an F-1 visa, an unappealed academic misconduct finding does more than hurt your GPA—it triggers automated SEVIS record termination and immediate immigration vulnerability. Under institutional bylaws, signing an "informal resolution" or accepting a summary sanction during a Dean meeting permanently waives your right to a formal appeal.</p>
      
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The Immigration Trap:</strong> A misconduct flag on an academic transcript can prevent I-20 extensions, visa renewals, or practical training (OPT) approvals.</li>
        <li><strong>The Waiver Risk:</strong> Agreeing to "just take a zero" is legally logged as a confession of academic dishonesty, blocking downstream appeals.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Main Actionable Walkthrough</h2>
      <p>To survive a Dean meeting, replace emotional defense with objective, chronological evidence structuring:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Chronological Evidence Binder:</strong> Collect original assignment prompts, brainstorming outlines, and early draft exports.</li>
        <li><strong>Cloud Revision Logs:</strong> Export raw telemetry data via Google Docs Version History or Word Track Changes showing active drafting velocity.</li>
        <li><strong>Syllabus Indexing:</strong> Cross-reference course policies and stated guidelines against department allegations.</li>
      </ul>

